The Bacchae is a Greek tragedy by Euripides, first performed in 405 BC. It tells the story of King Pentheus of Thebes, who refuses to acknowledge the return of the god Dionysus to his city. Dionysus takes revenge by driving Pentheus and the women of Thebes into a frenzy, during which they tear Pentheus apart. The play explores themes of religious fanaticism, the conflict between tradition and innovation, and the dangers of underestimating the power of the divine.
